chris: FYI customer reports duplicate POSTs from our TS SDK after upgrading to Node 20 + undici. They see two orders created even though the app only called once.
maria: Yikes. Can you paste a minimal repro and the request/response logs?
chris: Repro is pretty small. We wrap client calls in a retry helper and were reusing the same AbortController across attempts. Snippet: 
```js
// simplified
const ac = new AbortController();
for (let i=0;i<3;i++) {
  try {
    await client.requests.post('/orders', { body: JSON.stringify(payload), signal: ac.signal });
    break;
  } catch (e) {
    await delay(backoff(i));
  }
}
```
chris: Customer logs: initial POST returned 200 (order created), then SDK saw a network error and retried, final attempt ended up 200 again with a different order id. They ended up with two orders.
ben: That pattern screams signal/body reuse + undici behavior difference in Node 20. If the body stream isn't rewindable and we re-use the same request init, undici/node fetch semantics can cause unexpected client-side failures and duplicate server processing.
maria: Which SDK version + Node exact? Any proxy/loadbalancer in front?
chris: SDK 2.3.1, Node 20.4.0 (global fetch = undici), no extra proxy (customer on VPC). undici is transitive. We saw a 200 -> ECONNRESET -> 200 sequence in the HTTP logs:
```
[2026-03-15T12:01:05Z] POST /orders  200  x-req-id=order-abc
[2026-03-15T12:01:05Z] connection reset by peer (client reported)
[2026-03-15T12:01:06Z] POST /orders 500  x-req-id=order-abc
[2026-03-15T12:01:07Z] POST /orders 200  x-req-id=order-def
```
chris: The odd part: the second log line shows a 500 for the same x-req-id then a final 200 with a different id. Customer's app saw an error then a success.
ben: Two things to try/workaround: 1) ensure a new AbortController per attempt (don't reuse the same signal) 2) serialize the body into a Buffer/string and reconstruct per attempt (e.g., Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(payload))) so the body is replayable. Also ask them to include an Idempotency-Key header for POSTs.
maria: Can we reproduce locally? If so we should ship a patch to the SDK to avoid reusing the same signal and to clone JSON bodies automatically when running under Node+undici.
chris: I reproduced with a tiny express server that intentionally resets the socket during the first send. Repro steps in case you want to run it:
1) Node 20.4.0
2) run server.js (attached below) that returns 200 then forcibly closes socket on second chunk
3) run the client snippet that uses our SDK with retry wrapper

server.js:
```js
const http = require('http');
let count = 0;
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  count++ ;
  if (count === 1) {
    res.writeHead(200, {'content-type':'application/json'});
    res.write(JSON.stringify({id:'abc'}));
    res.end();
  } else if (count === 2) {
    // simulate server error during streaming response
    req.socket.destroy(); // abrupt close
  } else {
    res.writeHead(200, {'content-type':'application/json'});
    res.end(JSON.stringify({id:'def'}));
  }
}).listen(3001);
```
chris: Local run reproduces duplicate creates.
ben: Thanks — confirmed locally. Quick patch idea: in the SDK request pipeline, allocate a fresh AbortController for each retry attempt and, when the body is a plain object/string, ensure we recreate the init.body on retry instead of reusing the same stream/Readable. I'll draft a PR.
maria: For the immediate customer reply: ask them to 1) set SDK retries to 0, 2) add an Idempotency-Key header (recommended), and 3) if they can, patch their retry wrapper to new AbortController per attempt or switch to SDK v2.4.x which includes the upcoming fix.
chris: Customer prefers quick fix — I'll offer the retry=0 + idempotency keyed approach and include the repro steps so they can validate.
ben: I'll add unit/integration tests that run under node:20 in CI matrix. Also will add a note to docs about body replayability and Node 20 undici semantics.
